@page "/exgrid/sorting-filtering"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Sorting, filtering and find"
          Lead="The grid never sorts, filters or searches rows itself: it does not hold them. It offers the gestures, and asks whoever holds the rows to answer. GridSource.From answers for a list in memory, and its answers are the reference for what each one means.">

    <DocsSection Title="With GridSource.From">
        <p>
            Bind a <code>GridSource.From(rows)</code> and everything on this page works with no further code:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Click a header to sort.</strong> One click sorts ascending, a second descending, a third
                returns to your own order. A click replaces the whole sort with that one column; a multi-column
                sort is expressed in code, as a list of <code>SortSpec</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Open the ▾ in a header</strong> (or press <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>↓</kbd>) for the column menu
                and the filter panel. A column declares which panel it offers with <code>filterUi</code>: a
                <code>ValueList</code> of distinct values to tick, a <code>Condition</code> (the default), or
                <code>Both</code>. The value list is asked for when the panel opens; past 1,000 distinct values it
                gives way to the condition form.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd></strong> in the grid for the find panel. It searches
                every row of the result, not only the painted ones, and matches the text each cell shows; it
                searches the column headers too, below. With several cells selected, it searches the selection. <kbd>Enter</kbd> in its field goes to the
                next match, and <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> to the previous one. If the rows move, or another
                <code>Source</code> is handed over, while a step is out, its answer moves nothing: the panel says
                to find again, and <code>OnFindRefused</code> says <code>OrderChanged</code> or
                <code>SourceChanged</code>.
                Characters and completed IME compositions typed as the panel opens reach its text field in
                order, including over Blazor Server. Ticking <em>Match case</em> or <em>Match entire cell
                contents</em> takes its place in that order too, with the steps and the keys: a step clicked
                straight after a tick searches with the option ticked, and one clicked before it with the option
                as it stood, however fast they come and over any round trip.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>While the panel holds a search, every matching cell on screen is marked</strong>, as you
                type, and the current match (the cell the last step moved to) is marked apart. Rows you scroll
                to are marked as they appear, and the marks go when the panel closes or its field is emptied.
                Only the cells on screen are judged, so nothing is counted: the grid shows no "3 of 40". The
                grid judges those cells itself, by the same rule <code>GridSource.From</code> finds by, so the
                marks follow the scroll with no round trip. Template cells are searched and marked like any
                other cell with a value; action buttons are not. The colours are the Visual Tokens
                <code>--ex-match-mark-fill</code> and <code>--ex-match-mark-current-fill</code>, on the classes
                <code>ex-match-mark</code> and <code>ex-match-mark-current</code>; <code>ExGrid.MudBlazor</code>
                maps them to the palette's secondary and tertiary.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Title="Sort, filter and find" Builtin="typeof(SortFilterFind)" Mud="typeof(SortFilterFindMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Find in the headers">
        <p>
            <strong>Find searches the column headers and the Header Group labels too</strong>, as they are shown,
            on every grid and with nothing to wire. A search for "price" in a grid whose cells never say it stops
            on the Price header, rather than answering "No match" for a word that is on the screen.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>The headers come before the first row.</strong> A step walks the top Header Group row
                first, down to the column header row, each left to right in the order the columns stand now, and
                then the rows; past the last row it comes back to the headers. <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>
                walks the other way. A search whose only match is a header finds it again on every step.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A header found is the current match, and the Focus stays where it is</strong>: a header is
                not a cell, and moving the Focus to a cell under it would show something that did not match. The
                header wears the current match's mark, is scrolled into view across, and the panel names it —
                "Found in the header Trader" — which a screen reader hears, as the header itself says it is the
                current one (<code>aria-current</code>). The next step goes on from that header, wherever the
                columns have moved it meanwhile. Selecting a cell leaves it, and the panel says no more of it;
                the next step starts from your Focus. A substituted Chrome reads the header's text from
                <code>FindContext.FoundHeader</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Every header on screen that matches is marked</strong>, as matching cells are, with the
                same two Visual Tokens. The grid judges the headers itself, by the rule <code>GridSource.From</code>
                finds by, over the text the header shows; it holds that text, so this needs no
                <code>FindMatch</code> of yours, and headers are marked even where your own <code>OnFind</code>
                answers and no cell is.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Not searched:</strong> a hidden column's header, the headers of a grid that hides them
                (<code>HideHeader</code>), and any header while several cells are selected, since the selection
                as the scope is cells. Replace never writes a header: with one as the current match it finds the
                next match, and Replace All asks about cells only.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>FindsHeaders="false"</code></strong> turns this off, for headers that are addresses
                rather than content. ExSheet does, so its column letters are never found.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Your answer to a step is unchanged: the request still names a cell, or none, and you still answer with a
            cell or "not found". The grid reads whether your answer wrapped past the end (or the start, backward)
            and, when it did, or found nothing, stops on the first matching header (the last, backward) instead.
            From a header, it asks you from the start of the rows (the end, backward).
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Find in the headers" Builtin="typeof(FindHeaders)" Mud="typeof(FindHeadersMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Replace">
        <p>
            <strong>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd></strong> for the find panel with Replace, as Excel's dialog
            has both. It is offered where a replacement can land: you pass <code>OnReplace</code>, at least one
            visible column is Editable, and Find is wired. Anywhere else <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd> is still taken
            from the browser (whose own key opens its history), opens nothing, and <code>OnFindRefused</code> says
            <code>ReplaceUnavailable</code>, as <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> is refused where nothing can search.
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> turns the panel back into Find alone.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>What is matched is the cell's entry</strong>: the text the Cell Editor opens with
                (<code>EditorTextOf</code>'s, or the text the cell shows, but a <code>double</code> with no Format whole),
                not only what it shows. Match case,
                Whole cell and the selection as the scope are Find's own options, carried over. Every occurrence
                in the entry is replaced; with Whole cell, the entry whole.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Replace</strong> writes the current match (the cell the last step found, while the Focus
                stays there) and then finds the next; a header as the current match is never written, and the
                press only finds the next. It is an ordinary write of that one cell: an
                <code>OnEdit</code> Edit Intent, with the column's Edit Verdict. With no current match it finds one
                first, so you see what the next press writes. A match in a column that is not Editable, a Reject,
                or an intent you <code>Refuse</code> writes nothing, and the panel says which cell and why.
                Replace moves as Find's steps do, so it reaches the cells whose shown text matches; a cell whose
                entry matches and whose shown text does not (a number shown formatted) is reached by Replace All
                only, and one whose shown text matches and whose entry does not is passed over.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Replace All</strong> is one <code>GridReplaceRequest</code>: Find's text and options, the
                replacement, the scope, the visible columns, which of them are Editable, and the
                <code>RowSequenceVersion</code> it was read in. The grid writes nothing: you write every match
                <strong>or none</strong>, as one step of your undo stack, and answer with the count
                (<code>GridReplaceResult.Replaced</code>) or the cells you could not write and why
                (<code>GridReplaceResult.Refused</code>). There is no cap. The panel says "Replaced 40 cells.", or
                "Nothing was replaced." and names the first five cells, counting the rest. You answer under the
                version you read your rows in; an answer under another version than the request's is refused as
                the rows having moved (<code>OrderChanged</code>), and you must have written nothing then.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>GridSource.From</code> answers it for you</strong>: hand its <code>Replace</code> the
                request and your write of one cell — the same write your <code>OnEdit</code> makes, returning a
                new row instance or a refusal — and it plans every match, applies the rewritten rows at once if
                none refused, and gives you the result for the grid and the rows it changed for your undo stack.
                <code>GridReplace.Plan</code> is that reference implementation, for a server that answers itself.
                In ExGrid for React it is <code>GridSource.from</code>'s <code>replace</code>, whose answer's
                <code>result</code> goes back to the grid's <code>onReplace</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                The panel's Replace field and its two buttons take their place in the order of input with the
                grid's keys and presses, as Find's steps do, so a click typed ahead of a round trip is never
                handled out of turn. <code>ExGrid.MudBlazor</code> draws them as a <code>MudTextField</code> and
                two <code>MudButton</code>s.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Title="Find and replace" Builtin="typeof(FindReplace)" Mud="typeof(FindReplaceMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What sorting and filtering mean">
        <p>
            <code>GridSource.From</code> is the reference implementation: a server that sorts or filters for the
            grid should answer exactly as it does. The rules are short:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Text compares <strong>ordinally, ignoring case</strong>, everywhere: the same answer on every machine and in every culture, and one a database can match.</li>
            <li>A <strong>Blank</strong> is a value accessor returning <code>null</code>. An empty string is a value, not a Blank.</li>
            <li><strong>Blanks sort last</strong> in both directions, as in Excel, and the sort is stable.</li>
            <li>A Blank matches only <code>IsBlank</code>, or a value list that ticks it. <code>NotEquals</code> and <code>DoesNotContain</code> do not match a Blank.</li>
            <li>Which operators a column offers follows its type: <code>Contains</code> and <code>StartsWith</code> for text, <code>GreaterThan</code> and friends for numbers and dates. The grid decides it, not the filter panel: a panel of your own renders the operators its context's <code>Allowed</code> lists, and one it hands back that is not among them is refused, with the panel left open.</li>
            <li>Anything ambiguous is <strong>refused with an exception naming the column</strong> — an unknown column, an operator the type does not allow, a value of the wrong type — rather than guessed at.</li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Sorting and filtering from code">
        <p>
            The sort and filter are plain data — <code>SortSpec</code> and <code>GridFilter</code> — so a toolbar
            button, a saved view or a link can set them. Call the source's <code>OnSortChanged</code> and
            <code>OnFilterChanged</code>; the header's sort arrow and the filter panel follow, and the source raises
            <code>StateChanged</code> whenever the result changes, whoever changed it. In ExGrid for React the same
            source takes <code>setSorts</code> and <code>setFilter</code>, and <code>subscribe</code> tells you of each
            change, so <code>useSyncExternalStore</code> over its <code>snapshot</code> keeps a line like the one
            below in step.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A saved view" Builtin="typeof(QueryFromCode)" Mud="typeof(QueryFromCodeMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="When you hold the rows yourself">
        <p>
            Without a Source you push the rows (see <a href="exgrid/data">Data sources</a>), and the gestures arrive
            as notifications. Pass <code>Sorts</code> and <code>Filters</code> so the header and panel show what is in
            force, answer <code>OnSortChanged</code> and <code>OnFilterChanged</code> with a new <code>Window</code>,
            <code>OnDistinctValuesNeeded</code> for the value list, and <code>OnFind</code> for
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>. <code>GridQueryEngine.Apply</code> and <code>GridFind.Step</code> are the
            reference implementations, if you want to run them over rows of your own.
        </p>
        <p>
            Bump <code>RowSequenceVersion</code> whenever the order of the rows changes. The grid holds only the
            Window and cannot see a reorder; the version is how it knows to drop a selection that would now cover
            other rows.
        </p>
        <p>
            Where nothing can answer a find, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> is <strong>refused</strong> rather than
            left to the browser, whose find sees only the rows on screen and would report "no match" for a row a
            little further down.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>When you answer the find yourself — with <code>OnFind</code>, with the <code>find</code> delegate
            of <code>GridSource.Fetch</code>, or with a Source of your own — the matches on screen are marked only if
            you pass <code>FindMatch</code></strong>: the rule your answer finds by, asked of each cell on screen with the
            text and the two options the panel holds (a <code>GridFindPattern</code>). Your search may not be the
            reference one, and a mark your next step does not reach would be wrong, so without it nothing is marked.
            If you find by the reference rule, pass it over the columns' displayed text,
            <code>(row, column, pattern) =&gt; GridFind.Matches(column.Info.TextOf(row), pattern)</code>, and hold
            the function in a field: a new one makes every row on screen judge its cells again.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>A step you cannot answer is given up, not answered "no match".</strong> When your search cannot
            read what it searches — a server refused, or the data moved under it — throw
            <code>OperationCanceledException</code> from <code>OnFind</code>: the Focus does not move, the panel stays
            open and says nothing, and you say why yourself, as ExPivot does on its toolbar. Answering
            <code>NotFound</code> instead would claim that cells nobody read hold no match.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Your own find, and its marks" Builtin="typeof(FindMarks)" Mud="typeof(FindMarksMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

